#let chapter = [
  #[
    #import "main-defs.typ": L, chapter-ref, qed, section-ref, source
    #source(94, "81")
    = The Subgroups $lr(⟨X_r, X_(-r)⟩)$ <ch:root-sl2-subgroups>
    In the preceding chapter we considered relations involving generators
    $x_r (t),x_s (u)$ of a Chevalley group corresponding to linearly independent
    roots $r,s$, and showed in particular that two such elements generate a
    nilpotent subgroup. We shall now investigate relations involving generators
    of the form $x_r (t),x_(-r)(u)$. We do this by determining the structure of
    the subgroup $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$ generated by two root subgroups
    corresponding to opposite roots. This subgroup turns out to be closely
    related to the group $italic("SL")_2(K)$; in fact there is a homomorphism
    from $italic("SL")_2(K)$ onto $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$. We prove the existence of
    this homomorphism first for the complex field. Regarding #L as a module for
    the group $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$, we show that the operation of
    $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$ on certain submodules of #L is the same as the operation
    of $italic("SL")_2(CC)$ on the space of homogeneous polynomials in two
    variables of a suitable degree.#footnote[This method of establishing the
      existence of the homomorphism was described to the author by C. W.
      Curtis.]

    == The Group $italic("SL")_2(K)$ <sec:group-sl2>
    #metadata((kind: "anchor")) <l:6-1-1>
    #smallcaps[Lemma 6.1.1.] _Let $K$ be an arbitrary field. Then the group
    $italic("SL")_2(K)$ is generated by the elements_
    $ mat(1, t; 0, 1), quad mat(1, 0; t, 1) $ <eq:6-1-1>
    _as $t$ runs through $K$._

    #smallcaps[Proof.] Let
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-1-2>
    be an element of $italic("SL")_2(K)$. If $gamma!=0$ we have
    $
      mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) = mat(1, (alpha-1)gamma^(-1); 0, 1) mat(
        1, 0;
        gamma, 1
      ) mat(1, (delta-1)gamma^(-1); 0, 1).
    $ <eq:6-1-1-factorization>

    #source(95, "82")
    #block(sticky: true)[If $beta!=0$ we have]
    $
      mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta)
      =mat(1, 0; (delta-1)beta^(-1), 1)
      mat(1, beta; 0, 1)mat(1, 0; (alpha-1)beta^(-1), 1).
    $ <eq:6-1-1-beta-factorization>
    #block(sticky: true)[If $beta=gamma=0$ we have]
    // E026: bottom-right entry of the last factor is misprinted as 0.
    $
      mat(alpha, 0; 0, alpha^(-1))
      =mat(1, 0; alpha^(-1)-1, 1)mat(1, 1; 0, 1)
      mat(1, 0; alpha-1, 1)mat(1, -alpha^(-1); 0, 1).
    $ <eq:6-1-1-diagonal-factorization>
    It is clear from these relations that $S L_2(K)$ is generated in the manner
    described.#linebreak()#qed

    #block(sticky: true)[
      Let $R=CC[x,y]$ be the polynomial ring in two variables over $CC$ and
      $R_q$ be the subspace of polynomials which are homogeneous of degree $q$.
      $R_q$ has a basis
    ]
    $ x^q,x^(q-1)y,x^(q-2)y^2,dots,y^q $ <eq:6-1-polynomial-basis>
    #block(sticky: true)[and dimension $q+1$. An element]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-1-polynomial-matrix>
    // E027: the original says SL_2(K), although R is C[x,y].
    #block(sticky: true)[
      of $S L_2(CC)$ operates on $R$ by the transformation
    ]
    $
      x & arrow.r alpha x+gamma y, \
      y & arrow.r beta x+delta y,
    $ <eq:6-1-polynomial-action>
    and $R_q$ is a representation space for $S L_2(CC)$ for each $q$ under this
    operation.

    #metadata((kind: "anchor")) <l:6-1-2>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      #smallcaps[Lemma 6.1.2.] _Let $v_i=x^i y^(q-i)$, $i=0,1,dots,q$. Then the
      images of $v_i$ under transformation by_
    ]
    $ mat(1, t; 0, 1) quad "and" quad mat(1, 0; t, 1) $
    <eq:6-1-2-generators>
    #block(sticky: true)[_are given by:_]
    $
      mat(1, t; 0, 1) dot v_i & =sum_(j=0)^(q-i) binom(q-i, j)t^j v_(i+j), \
      mat(1, 0; t, 1) dot v_i & =sum_(j=0)^i binom(i, j)t^j v_(i-j).
    $ <eq:6-1-2-actions>

    #source(96, "83")
    #block()[
      #block(sticky: true)[#smallcaps[Proof.] Since]
      $ mat(1, t; 0, 1) $ <eq:6-1-2-upper>
      #block(sticky: true)[
        transforms $x$ into itself and $y$ into $t x+y$, and
      ]
      $ mat(1, 0; t, 1) $ <eq:6-1-2-lower>
      transforms $y$ into itself and $x$ into $x+t y$, the result is clear. #qed

    ]
  ]

