  #[
    #import "main-defs.typ": L, book-ref, qed, section-ref, source

    == The Homomorphism from $S L_2(K)$ <sec:homomorphism-from-sl2>

    We now transfer from the complex field to an arbitrary field $K$ and
    establish the following result.

    #source(101, "88")
    #metadata((kind: "anchor")) <th:6-3-1>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      #smallcaps[Theorem 6.3.1.] _Let $K$ be any field. Then there is a
      homomorphism from $S L_2(K)$ onto the subgroup $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$ of
      $G=frak(L)(K)$ under which_
    ]
    $
      mat(1, t; 0, 1) & arrow.r x_r (t), \
      mat(1, 0; t, 1) & arrow.r x_(-r)(t).
    $ <eq:6-3-1-generator-images>

    #block(sticky: true)[
      #smallcaps[Proof.] We already have the existence of such a homomorphism
      when $K=CC$. Consider the module $frak(M)$ for $S L_2(CC)$ constructed in
      #section-ref("homomorphism-from-complex-sl2"). $frak(M)$ is a direct sum
      of modules $R_q$ with a trivial module, and we obtain a basis for
      $frak(M)$ by combining the natural bases of the components $R_q$ with a
      basis of the trivial module. With respect to this basis of $frak(M)$ an
      element
    ]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-3-1-complex-matrix>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      of $S L_2(CC)$ is represented by a matrix whose entries are polynomials in
      $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ with coefficients in $ZZ$. This is clear because
      each component $R_q$ gives such a polynomial representation. This basis
      for $frak(M)$ corresponds to a basis for #L as in #section-ref(
        "homomorphism-from-complex-sl2",
      ). This basis for #L is adapted to the decomposition
    ]
    $
      frak(L)=frak(H)_r^perp ⊕ frak(M)_r ⊕ sum_(s in Gamma)frak(M)_s
    $ <eq:6-3-1-decomposition>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      of #book-ref("p", "6-2-5", [6.2.5]), but is not necessarily a Chevalley
      basis of #L. However, it differs from a Chevalley basis only on $frak(H)$.
      It will be necessary to show that when we change to a basis of $frak(M)$
      corresponding to a Chevalley basis of #L, the element
    ]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-3-1-chevalley-matrix>
    of $S L_2(CC)$ will still be represented by a matrix
    $F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)$ whose entries are polynomials in
    $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ with coefficients in $ZZ$.

    With this in mind we first transform the basis elements of #L which lie in
    $frak(H)$ by an element of the Weyl group which transforms $h_r$ into a
    fundamental co-root. Since the elements of the Weyl group operate on
    $frak(H)$ by integral unimodular transformations, this will not affect the
    property #source(102, "89")with which we are concerned. Thus we may assume
    that $h_r$ is a fundamental co-root.

    #block(sticky: true)[
      Now the remaining elements of the Chevalley basis which lie in $frak(H)$
      are the other fundamental co-roots. Let $h_s$, $s in Pi$, be one of these.
      Then we have
    ]
    $ h_s=lambda dot h_r+y, $ <eq:6-3-1-coroot-decomposition>
    #block(sticky: true)[where $y in frak(H)_r^perp$ and]
    $ lambda=frac((h_r,h_s), (h_r,h_r))=frac((s,r), (s,s)). $
    <eq:6-3-1-coroot-coefficient>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      In particular, $2lambda$ is an integer. We note that if
      $theta in lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$ then we have
    ]
    $ theta(h_s)=lambda theta(h_r)+y=h_s+lambda(theta(h_r)-h_r). $
    <eq:6-3-1-coroot-action>

    #block(sticky: true)[
      Now consider the action of $S L_2(CC)$ on $frak(M)$. Let $m_r,m_s$ be
      basis elements of $frak(M)$ corresponding to $h_r,h_s in frak(L)$. Let
    ]
    $ T=mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) in S L_2(CC). $ <eq:6-3-1-t>
    #block(sticky: true)[Then we have]
    $ T(m_s)=m_s+lambda dot (T(m_r)-m_r). $ <eq:6-3-1-basis-action>
    By #book-ref("l", "6-2-4", [6.2.4]), $T(m_r)-m_r$ is a linear combination of
    basis elements of $frak(M)$ with coefficients which are polynomials in
    $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ with _even_ integer coefficients. Since $2lambda$
    is an integer the entries in the matrix $F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)$ are
    polynomials in $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ with coefficients in $ZZ$.

    #block(sticky: true)[
      We also observe from #book-ref("l", "6-2-4", [6.2.4]) that the polynomials
      in $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ occurring as coefficients in $T(m_r)-m_r$ are
      all homogeneous of degree $2$. Since $alpha delta-beta gamma=1$ we may
      write
    ]
    $ T(m_s)=(alpha delta-beta gamma)m_s+lambda dot (T(m_r)-m_r). $
    <eq:6-3-1-homogeneous-action>
    Thus $T(m_s)$ is a linear combination of basis elements of $frak(M)$ with
    coefficients which may be taken as homogeneous polynomials in
    $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ of degree $2$.

    #block(sticky: true)[We now transfer to the field $K$ and define a map]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) arrow.r F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta), $
    <eq:6-3-1-polynomial-map>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      where $alpha,beta,gamma,delta in K$. This maps $S L_2(K)$ into a group of
      matrices over $K$ obtained by replacing the variables in the polynomial
      entries of $F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)$ #source(103, "90")by elements of
      $K$. The condition for this map to be a homomorphism is the vanishing of
      the matrix
    ]
    $
      & F(alpha alpha'+beta gamma',alpha beta'+beta delta',
          gamma alpha'+delta gamma',gamma beta'+delta delta') \
      & quad -F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)F(alpha',beta',gamma',delta')
    $ <eq:6-3-1-homomorphism-condition>
    for all $alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta' in K$ satisfying
    $alpha delta-beta gamma=1$, $alpha' delta'-beta' gamma'=1$. This matrix
    certainly vanishes for all
    $alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta' in CC$ satisfying
    $alpha delta-beta gamma=1$, $alpha' delta'-beta' gamma'=1$, and we shall
    prove that the same is true for $K$.

    We take a Chevalley basis of #L and decompose it into disjoint subsets
    consisting of:

    (i) the root vectors corresponding to the roots in a given $r$-chain.

    #block(sticky: true)[
      (ii) the remaining set $e_r,e_(-r),h_s$ for $s in Pi$. The basis obtained
      for $frak(M)$ corresponding to this Chevalley basis of #L decomposes in an
      analogous way, and the submodules spanned by the basis vectors in each
      individual subset are invariant under $S L_2(CC)$. The matrix representing
    ]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-3-1-chain-matrix>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      on a submodule corresponding to an $r$-chain of roots of length $q+1$ has
      as its coefficients homogeneous polynomials in $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ of
      degree $q$, and the matrix representing
    ]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-3-1-cartan-matrix>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      on the submodule corresponding to ${e_r,e_(-r),h_s; s in Pi}$ has
      coefficients which are homogeneous polynomials in $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$
      of degree $2$, as has been pointed out above. Thus the matrix
      $F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)$ representing
    ]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) $ <eq:6-3-1-block-matrix>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      on $frak(M)$ is a diagonal sum of matrices $F_i (alpha,beta,gamma,delta)$
      in which the coefficients are homogeneous polynomials in
      $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ of constant degree. Also the matrix
    ]
    $
      & G(alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta') \
      & quad =F(alpha alpha'+beta gamma',alpha beta'+beta delta',
          gamma alpha'+delta gamma',gamma beta'+delta delta') \
      & quad quad -F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)F(alpha',beta',gamma',delta')
    $ <eq:6-3-1-g>
    is a diagonal sum of matrices
    $G_i (alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta')$ in which the
    coefficients are homogeneous polynomials of constant degree in each of
    $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ and $alpha',beta',gamma',delta'$ separately.

    #source(104, "91")
    #block(sticky: true)[Since $frak(M)$ is a module for $S L_2(CC)$, we have]
    $ G_i (alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta')=0 $
    <eq:6-3-1-g-vanishing>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      whenever $alpha delta-beta gamma=1$, $alpha' delta'-beta' gamma'=1$. Since
      $G_i$ is homogeneous in $alpha,beta,gamma,delta$ and also in
      $alpha',beta',gamma',delta'$, we have $G_i=0$ whenever
      $alpha delta-beta gamma=xi$, $alpha' delta'-beta' gamma'=xi'$, for all
      $xi,xi'!=0$. Let $P(alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta')$ be
      a matrix coefficient of $G_i$. Then the polynomial
    ]
    $
      & P(alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta') \
      & quad dot (alpha delta-beta gamma)(alpha' delta'-beta' gamma')
    $ <eq:6-3-1-p-vanishing>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      vanishes for all
      $alpha,beta,gamma,delta,alpha',beta',gamma',delta' in CC$, so is
      identically zero. Since $alpha delta-beta gamma$ and
      $alpha' delta'-beta' gamma'$ are not identically zero, $P$ is identically
      zero. Thus the matrix $G$ is identically zero and we have
    ]
    $
      & F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)F(alpha',beta',gamma',delta') \
      & quad equiv F(alpha alpha'+beta gamma',alpha beta'+beta delta',
          gamma alpha'+delta gamma',gamma beta'+delta delta')
    $ <eq:6-3-1-polynomial-identity>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      in the sense that equating matrix coefficients gives a polynomial
      identity. This remains true when we transfer to the field $K$. Thus the
      map
    ]
    $ mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) arrow.r F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta) $
    <eq:6-3-1-field-homomorphism>
    with $alpha,beta,gamma,delta in K$ is a homomorphism.

    #block(sticky: true)[Let]
    $
        A_r (zeta) & =F(1,zeta,0,1), \
      A_(-r)(zeta) & =F(1,0,zeta,1), quad zeta in CC.
    $ <eq:6-3-1-complex-generators>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      Then $A_r (zeta),A_(-r)(zeta)$ are the matrices representing
      $x_r (zeta),x_(-r)(zeta)$ with respect to the Chevalley basis of #L. For
      each $t in K$ let $A_r (t),A_(-r)(t)$ be the matrices obtained by
      replacing the complex variable $zeta$ in $A_r (zeta),A_(-r)(zeta)$ by the
      element $t$ of $K$. Then $A_r (t),A_(-r)(t)$ are the matrices representing
      the elements $x_r (t),x_(-r)(t)$ of $frak(L)(K)$, by definition of the
      latter. We have
    ]
    $
        A_r (t) & =F(1,t,0,1), \
      A_(-r)(t) & =F(1,0,t,1), quad "for" t in K.
    $ <eq:6-3-1-field-generators>
    #block(sticky: true)[Now $S L_2(K)$ is generated by the matrices]
    $ mat(1, t; 0, 1), quad mat(1, 0; t, 1) $ <eq:6-3-1-sl2-generators>
    #block(sticky: true)[
      for $t in K$ and so the group of matrices $F(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)$ with
      $alpha,beta,gamma,delta in K$ and $alpha delta-beta gamma=1$ is generated
      by $A_r (t),A_(-r)(t)$ for $t in K$. This group of matrices #source(
        105,
        "92",
      )is therefore isomorphic to the subgroup $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$ of
      $frak(L)(K)$. Thus we have a homomorphism from $S L_2(K)$ onto
      $lr(⟨X_r,X_(-r)⟩)$ under which
    ]
    $
      mat(1, t; 0, 1) arrow.r x_r (t) quad "and" quad
      mat(1, 0; t, 1) arrow.r x_(-r)(t).
    $ <eq:6-3-1-conclusion>
    This completes the proof. #qed
  ]

